    "body": "These lines are valid Python, but Cython cannot compile them. The last three are the cases from #3555 by @da-woods:\n\n| Code | Cython today |\n|---|---|\n| `a, b, c = x, *y` | `need more than 2 values to unpack` |\n| `a, b = x, *y` | `starred expression is not allowed here` |\n| `a, = *x, *y` | `too many values to unpack (expected 1, got 2)` |\n| `a, *b, c = x, y, *z` | `starred expression is not allowed here` |\n| `a, b, *c = x, y` | compiler crash, `no starred arg found when splitting starred assignment` |\n\n`flatten_parallel_assignments` pairs the targets with the right-hand items at compile time, which treats `*y` as a single value. It also expects a starred target to line up with a right-hand item, which fails when the star comes after the last item.\n\nThese assignments now skip the flattening and use the normal unpacking at runtime. `a, *b, c = x, y` still takes the compile-time path, and real mismatches such as `a, b, c = x, y` still give the same compile error. `() = ()` also crashes the compiler today, before and after this change, and is not part of #3555.\n\nI ran the unpacking tests (`extended_unpacking_T235`, `extended_unpacking_T409`, `tupleassign`, `unpack`, `cascadedassignment`, `tests/errors/extended_unpacking*`) on Windows with Python 3.13. The full test suite did not run here, since I have no MSVC on this machine.\n\nCloses #3555\n\nAI disclosure: the change, the tests and this text were written by Claude Code (Claude Opus 5.5). Codex (GPT-6 Astra) and a separate Claude Code session (Claude Opus 5.5) reviewed them. I reviewed the change and the results.\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-11-03T08:09:13Z",
